{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"## 🔵 §6 — Allowing, Never Denying\n\nOne piece of housekeeping before we get under way, and it is not politeness.\n\n**The word `ingress` is about to mean something completely different, and cap"}},"session_id":"83cacd78-581a-41c8-91be-f69f5ec7911f","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"10d7966d-1977-4360-af70-6a3ee816fcef"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"italisation is the tell.** For four sections, `Ingress` has meant an API object and the controller that fulfils it. From here to the end of §7, lowercase `ingress` means **a direction of traffic**"}},"session_id":"83cacd78-581a-41c8-91be-f69f5ec7911f","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"61cde4b8-88f0-4327-8fa3-079b4e7596f6"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":": inbound, as opposed to `egress`, outbound. NetworkPolicy has nothing to do with the Ingress object. If you carry the old meaning into this section, you will spend §7 trying to work out how the Ingress controller fits"}},"session_id":"83cacd78-581a-41c8-91be-f69f5ec7911f","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"9b9dc1af-a401-43a7-af9b-adce07889c66"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" in, and it does not.\n\nGood. Now the object.\n\n### What a NetworkPolicy controls\n\n**NetworkPolicies let you specify rules for traffic flow at the IP address or port level — OSI layer 3 or 4 — within"}},"session_id":"83cacd78-581a-41c8-91be-f69f5ec7911f","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"58a74fc4-a71e-4a4b-a4c2-a0276ab4bb8a"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" your cluster, and also between Pods and the outside world** [source: k8s-docs-network-policies-depth-2026-08-24]. They are an **application-centric construct**, which lets you specify how a Pod is"}},"session_id":"83cacd78-581a-41c8-91be-f69f5ec7911f","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"a379c56d-48a0-4150-8d00-8d3b0be9312c"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" allowed to communicate with various network *entities* — a word the documentation chose deliberately to avoid overloading \"endpoints\" and \"services,\" which already have specific Kubernetes meanings [source: k8s-doc"}},"session_id":"83cacd78-581a-41c8-91be-f69f5ec7911f","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"946b6462-5370-48a1-bf3e-6f260ddafc92"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"s-network-policies-depth-2026-08-24]. And they **apply to a connection with a Pod on one or both ends, and are not relevant to other connections** [source: k8s-docs-network-policies-depth-2026-08-24].\n\nNote what that r"}},"session_id":"83cacd78-581a-41c8-91be-f69f5ec7911f","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"52151ea2-50b8-4cdd-acd6-c3911773f6b6"}
